WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:21.680
Well, this is a really extraordinary honor for me. I spend most of my time in jails and

00:21.680 --> 00:27.320
prisons on death row. I spend most of my time in very low-income communities and the projects

00:27.320 --> 00:33.360
and places where there's a great deal of hopelessness. And being here at TED and seeing the stimulation,

00:33.360 --> 00:37.680
hearing it has been very, very energizing to me. And one of the things that's emerged

00:37.680 --> 00:44.500
in my short time here is that TED has an identity. And you can actually say things here that

00:44.500 --> 00:48.520
have impacts around the world. And sometimes when it comes through TED, it has meaning

00:48.520 --> 00:54.560
and power that it doesn't have when it doesn't. And I mention that because I think identity

00:54.560 --> 01:00.240
is really important. And we've had some fantastic presentations. And I think what we've learned

01:00.240 --> 01:04.680
is that if you're a teacher, your words can be meaningful, but if you're a compassionate

01:04.680 --> 01:08.800
teacher, they can be especially meaningful. If you're a doctor, you can do some good things,

01:08.800 --> 01:13.080
but if you're a caring doctor, you can do some other things. So I want to talk about

01:13.080 --> 01:18.440
the power of identity. And I didn't learn about this actually practicing law and doing

01:18.440 --> 01:23.720
the work that I do. I actually learned about this from my grandmother. I grew up in a house

01:23.720 --> 01:28.000
that was the traditional African American home that was dominated by a matriarch, and

01:28.000 --> 01:34.760
that matriarch was my grandmother. She was tough. She was strong. She was powerful. She

01:34.760 --> 01:40.440
was the end of every argument in our family. She was the beginning of a lot of arguments

01:40.440 --> 01:45.360
in our family. She was the daughter of people who were actually enslaved. Her parents were

01:45.360 --> 01:49.960
born in slavery in Virginia in the 1840s. She was born in the 1880s, and the experience

01:49.960 --> 01:55.480
of slavery very much shaped the way she saw the world. My grandmother was tough, but she

01:55.480 --> 01:58.360
was also loving. When I would see her as a little boy, she'd come up to me, and she'd

01:58.360 --> 02:02.880
give me these hugs, and she'd squeeze me so tight I could barely breathe. And then she'd

02:02.880 --> 02:06.960
let me go. And an hour or two later, if I saw her, she'd come over to me, and she'd

02:06.960 --> 02:11.800
say, Brian, do you still feel me hugging you? And if I said no, she'd assault me again,

02:11.800 --> 02:15.440
and if I'd say yes, she'd leave me alone. And she just had this quality that you always

02:15.440 --> 02:20.240
wanted to be near her. And the only challenge was that she had 10 children. My mom was the

02:20.240 --> 02:23.600
youngest of her 10 kids, and sometimes when I would go and spend time with her, it would

02:23.600 --> 02:27.740
be difficult to get her time and attention. My cousins would be running around everywhere.

02:27.740 --> 02:32.360
And I remember when I was about 8 or 9 years old, waking up one morning, going into the

02:32.360 --> 02:36.360
living room, and all of my cousins were running around, and my grandmother was sitting across

02:36.360 --> 02:42.320
the room staring at me. And at first I thought we were playing a game. And I would look at

02:42.320 --> 02:47.120
her, and I'd smile, but she was very serious. And after about 15 or 20 minutes of this,

02:47.120 --> 02:51.920
she got up and she came across the room, and she took me by the hand, and she said, come

02:51.920 --> 02:56.200
on, Brian, you and I are going to have a talk. And I remember this just like it happened

02:56.200 --> 03:01.520
yesterday. I never will forget it. She took me out back, and she said, Brian, I'm going

03:01.520 --> 03:05.720
to tell you something, but you don't tell anybody what I tell you. I said, okay, mom.

03:05.720 --> 03:10.560
And she said, now you make sure you don't do that. I said, sure. Then she sat me down,

03:10.720 --> 03:16.720
and she looked at me, and she said, I want you to know I've been watching you. And she

03:16.720 --> 03:23.720
said, I think you're special. She said, I think you can do anything you want to do.

03:24.440 --> 03:29.400
I will never forget it. And then she said, I just need you to promise me three things,

03:29.400 --> 03:32.440
Brian. I said, okay, mom. And she said, the first thing I want you to promise me is that

03:32.440 --> 03:37.760
you'll always love your mom. She said, that's my baby girl, and you have to promise me now

03:37.800 --> 03:42.840
you'll always take care of her. Well, I adored my mom, so I said, yes, mama, I'll do that.

03:42.840 --> 03:45.600
Then she said, the second thing I want you to promise me is that you'll always do the

03:45.600 --> 03:51.600
right thing, even when the right thing is the hard thing. And I thought about it, and

03:51.600 --> 03:56.000
I said, yes, mama, I'll do that. Then finally she said, the third thing I want you to promise

03:56.000 --> 04:03.000
me is that you'll never drink alcohol. Well, I was nine years old, so I said, yes, mama,

04:03.000 --> 04:06.880
I'll do that. I grew up in the country in the rural south, and I have a brother a year

04:06.880 --> 04:11.400
older than me and a sister a year younger. When I was about 14 or 15, one day my brother

04:11.400 --> 04:15.240
came home, and he had this six-pack of beer. I don't know where he got it. And he grabbed

04:15.240 --> 04:18.480
me and my sister, and we went out in the woods, and we were kind of just out there doing the

04:18.480 --> 04:22.360
stuff we crazily did. And he had a sip of this beer, and he gave some to my sister,

04:22.360 --> 04:26.760
and she had some, and they offered it to me. I said, no, no, no, no, that's okay, y'all

04:26.760 --> 04:29.320
go ahead. I'm not going to have any beer. And my brother said, come on, we're doing

04:29.320 --> 04:32.720
this today. You always do what we do. I had some, your sister had some, have some beer.

04:32.720 --> 04:35.880
I said, no, I don't feel right about that. Y'all go ahead, y'all go ahead. And then my

04:35.880 --> 04:40.160
brother started staring at me. He said, what's wrong with you? Have some beer. Then he looked

04:40.160 --> 04:44.480
at me real hard. He said, oh, I hope you're not still hung up on that conversation Mama

04:44.480 --> 04:51.820
had with you. I said, well, what are you talking about? He says, oh, Mama tells all the grandkids

04:51.820 --> 05:02.000
that they're special. I was devastated. And I'm going to admit something to you. I'm going

05:02.000 --> 05:05.720
to tell you something I probably shouldn't. I know this might be broadcast broadly, but

05:05.720 --> 05:15.400
I'm 52 years old and I'm going to admit to you that I've never had a drop of alcohol.

05:15.400 --> 05:21.760
I don't say that because I think that's virtuous. I say that because there is power in identity.

05:21.760 --> 05:25.720
When we create the right kind of identity, we can say things to the world around us that

05:25.720 --> 05:30.160
they don't actually believe make sense. We can get them to do things that they don't

05:30.160 --> 05:34.680
think they can do. When I thought about my grandmother, of course she would think all

05:34.680 --> 05:38.780
her grandkids were special. My grandfather was in prison during prohibition. My male

05:38.780 --> 05:42.840
uncles died of alcohol-related diseases. And these were the things she thought we needed

05:42.840 --> 05:48.200
to commit to. Well, I've been trying to say something about our criminal justice system.

05:48.200 --> 05:54.220
This country is very different today than it was 40 years ago. In 1972, there were 300,000

05:54.220 --> 06:00.960
people in jails and prisons. Today, there are 2.3 million. The United States now has

06:00.960 --> 06:06.200
the highest rate of incarceration in the world. We have 7 million people on probation

06:06.200 --> 06:13.680
and parole. And mass incarceration, in my judgment, has fundamentally changed our world.

06:13.680 --> 06:18.960
In poor communities and communities of color, there is this despair, there is this hopelessness

06:18.960 --> 06:23.620
that is being shaped by these outcomes. One out of three black men between the ages of

06:23.620 --> 06:30.040
18 and 30 is in jail, in prison, on probation or parole. In urban communities across this

06:30.040 --> 06:35.680
country, Los Angeles, Philadelphia, Baltimore, Washington, 50 to 60 percent of all young

06:35.680 --> 06:41.080
men of color in jail or prison, or on probation and parole. Our system isn't just being shaped

06:41.080 --> 06:45.280
in these ways that seem to be distorting around race, they're also distorted by poverty. We

06:45.280 --> 06:50.940
have a system of justice in this country that treats you much better if you're rich and

06:50.940 --> 06:58.560
guilty than if you're poor and innocent. Wealth, not culpability, shapes outcomes. And yet

06:58.600 --> 07:04.480
we seem to be very comfortable. The politics of fear and anger has made us believe that

07:04.480 --> 07:10.940
these are problems that are not our problems. We've been disconnected. It's interesting

07:10.940 --> 07:15.200
to me, we're looking at some very interesting developments in our work. My state of Alabama,

07:15.200 --> 07:19.560
like a number of states, actually permanently disenfranchises you if you have a criminal

07:19.560 --> 07:25.140
conviction. Right now in Alabama, 34 percent of the black male population has permanently

07:25.140 --> 07:29.860
lost the right to vote. We're actually projecting in another 10 years the level of disenfranchisement

07:29.860 --> 07:34.220
will be as high as it's been since prior to the passage of the Voting Rights Act and

07:34.220 --> 07:41.220
there is this stunning silence. I represent children. A lot of my clients are very young.

07:41.900 --> 07:46.260
The United States is the only country in the world where we sentence 13-year-old children

07:46.260 --> 07:51.620
to die in prison. We have life imprisonment without parole for kids in this country. We're

07:51.620 --> 07:56.300
actually doing some litigation. The only country in the world. I represent people on

07:56.300 --> 08:00.660
death row. It's interesting, this question of the death penalty. In many ways, we've

08:00.660 --> 08:05.300
been taught to think that the real question is, do people deserve to die for the crimes

08:05.300 --> 08:09.560
they've committed? And that's a very sensible question. But there's another way of thinking

08:09.560 --> 08:14.340
about where we are in our identity. The other way of thinking about it is not do people

08:14.340 --> 08:20.140
deserve to die for the crimes they commit, but do we deserve to kill? I mean, it's fascinating.

08:20.140 --> 08:24.900
Death penalty in America is defined by error. For every nine people who have been executed,

08:24.900 --> 08:29.460
we've actually identified one innocent person who's been exonerated and released from death

08:29.460 --> 08:36.460
row. A kind of astonishing error rate. One out of nine people. Innocent. I mean, it's

08:36.540 --> 08:41.800
fascinating in aviation, we would never let people fly on airplanes if for every nine

08:41.800 --> 08:47.060
planes that took off, one would crash. But somehow we can insulate ourselves from this

08:47.060 --> 08:54.060
problem. It's not our problem. It's not our burden. It's not our struggle. Well, I talk

08:54.220 --> 08:59.020
a lot about these issues. I talk about race and this question of whether we deserve to

08:59.020 --> 09:02.660
kill. And it's interesting, when I teach my students about African American history, I

09:02.660 --> 09:07.940
tell them about slavery. I tell them about terrorism, the era that began at the end of

09:07.940 --> 09:11.500
Reconstruction that went on to World War II. We don't really know very much about it, but

09:11.500 --> 09:17.560
for African Americans in this country, that was an error defined by terror. In many communities,

09:17.560 --> 09:20.500
people had to worry about being lynched. They had to worry about being bombed. It was the

09:20.500 --> 09:24.580
threat of terror that shaped their lives. And these older people come up to me now and

09:24.580 --> 09:28.940
they say, Mr. Stevenson, you give talks, you make speeches. You tell people to stop saying

09:28.940 --> 09:34.260
we're dealing with terrorism for the first time in our nation's history after 9-11. They

09:34.260 --> 09:39.180
tell me to say, no, tell them that we grew up with that. And that era of terrorism, of

09:39.180 --> 09:45.820
course, was followed by segregation and decades of racial subordination and apartheid. And

09:45.820 --> 09:51.340
yet we have in this country this dynamic where we really don't like to talk about our problems.

09:51.340 --> 09:56.820
We don't like to talk about our history. And because of that, we really haven't understood

09:56.820 --> 10:01.380
what it's meant to do the things we've done historically. We're constantly running into

10:01.380 --> 10:05.740
each other. We're constantly creating tensions and conflicts. We have a hard time talking

10:05.740 --> 10:11.380
about race. And I believe it's because we are unwilling to commit ourselves to a process

10:11.380 --> 10:16.480
of truth and reconciliation. In South Africa, people understood that we couldn't overcome

10:16.480 --> 10:20.940
apartheid without a commitment to truth and reconciliation. In Rwanda, even after the

10:20.940 --> 10:24.740
genocide, there was this commitment. But in this country we haven't done that. I was giving

10:24.740 --> 10:28.740
this lecture in Germany, some lectures in Germany about the death penalty. It was fascinating

10:28.740 --> 10:33.060
because one of the scholars stood up after the presentation and said, well, you know,

10:33.060 --> 10:37.220
it's deeply troubling to hear what you're talking about. I said, well, we don't have

10:37.220 --> 10:42.580
the death penalty in Germany. And of course, we can never have the death penalty in Germany.

10:42.580 --> 10:49.480
And the room got very quiet and this woman said, there's no way with our history we can

10:49.480 --> 10:57.180
ever engage in the systematic killing of human beings. It would be unconscionable for us

10:57.180 --> 11:04.500
to in an intentional, deliberate way set about executing people. And I thought about that.

11:04.500 --> 11:09.400
What would it feel like to be living in a world where the nation state of Germany was

11:09.400 --> 11:13.180
executing people, especially if they were disproportionately Jewish? I couldn't bear

11:13.180 --> 11:21.420
it. It would be unconscionable. And yet in this country, in the states of the old south,

11:21.420 --> 11:25.540
we execute people where you're 11 times more likely to get the death penalty if the victim

11:25.540 --> 11:29.140
is white than if the victim is black, 22 times more likely to get it if the defendant

11:29.140 --> 11:33.340
is black and the victim is white. In the very states where there are buried in the ground

11:33.340 --> 11:39.940
the bodies of people who were lynched. And yet there is this disconnect. Well, I believe

11:39.940 --> 11:47.460
that our identity is at risk. That when we actually don't care about these difficult

11:47.460 --> 11:56.100
things, the positive and wonderful things are nonetheless implicated. We love innovation.

11:56.100 --> 12:05.140
We love technology. We love creativity. We love entertainment. But ultimately those realities

12:05.140 --> 12:14.280
are shadowed by suffering, abuse, degradation, marginalization. And for me, it becomes necessary

12:14.280 --> 12:20.880
to integrate the two. Because ultimately we are talking about a need to be more hopeful,

12:20.880 --> 12:26.360
more committed, more dedicated to the basic challenges of living in a complex world. And

12:26.360 --> 12:32.380
for that means, for me that means spending time thinking and talking about the poor,

12:32.380 --> 12:37.920
the disadvantaged, those who will never get to TED. But thinking about them in a way that

12:37.920 --> 12:43.160
is integrated in our own lives. You know, ultimately we all have to believe things we

12:43.160 --> 12:49.320
haven't seen. We do. As rational as we are, as committed to intellect as we are, innovation,

12:49.320 --> 12:55.340
creativity, development comes not from the ideas in our mind alone. They come from the

12:55.340 --> 13:01.240
ideas in our mind that are also fueled by some conviction in our heart. And it's that

13:01.240 --> 13:08.400
mind-heart connection that I believe compels us to not just be attentive to all the bright

13:08.400 --> 13:14.240
and dazzling things, but also the dark and difficult things. Václav Havel, the great

13:14.240 --> 13:18.640
Czech leader, talked about this. He said, when we were in Eastern Europe and dealing

13:18.640 --> 13:23.720
with oppression, we wanted all kinds of things, but mostly what we needed was hope, an orientation

13:23.720 --> 13:29.240
of the spirit, a willingness to sometimes be in hopeless places and be a witness. Well,

13:29.240 --> 13:36.520
that orientation of the spirit is very much at the core of what I believe even TED communities

13:36.640 --> 13:43.240
have to be engaged in. There is no disconnect around technology and design that will allow

13:43.240 --> 13:50.000
us to be fully human until we pay attention to suffering, to poverty, to exclusion, to

13:50.000 --> 13:55.840
unfairness, to injustice. Now, I will warn you that this kind of identity is a much more

13:55.840 --> 14:01.840
challenging identity than ones that don't pay attention to this. It will get to you.

14:01.840 --> 14:06.280
I had the great privilege when I was a young lawyer meeting Rosa Parks, and Ms. Parks used

14:06.320 --> 14:09.520
to come back to Montgomery every now and then, and she would get together with two of her

14:09.520 --> 14:14.840
dearest friends, these older women, Johnny Carr, who was the organizer of the Montgomery

14:14.840 --> 14:20.000
Bus Boycott, an amazing African American woman, and Virginia Durr, a white woman, whose husband

14:20.000 --> 14:24.640
Clifford Durr represented Dr. King. And these women would get together and just talk. And

14:24.640 --> 14:28.380
every now and then Ms. Carr would call me, and she'd say, Brian, Ms. Parks is coming

14:28.380 --> 14:31.800
to town, and we're going to get together and talk. Do you want to come over and listen?

14:31.800 --> 14:35.520
And I'd say, yes, ma'am, I do. And she said, well, what are you going to do when you get

14:35.520 --> 14:39.440
here? I said, I'm going to listen. And I'd go over there, and I would. I would just listen.

14:39.440 --> 14:43.960
It would be so energizing and so empowering. And one time I was over there listening to

14:43.960 --> 14:47.600
these women talk, and after a couple of hours, Ms. Parks turned to me, and she said, now,

14:47.600 --> 14:52.320
Brian, tell me what the Equal Justice Initiative is. Tell me what you're trying to do. I began

14:52.320 --> 14:57.080
giving her my wrap. I said, well, we're trying to challenge injustice. We're trying to help

14:57.080 --> 15:01.000
people who have been wrongly convicted. We're trying to confront bias and discrimination

15:01.000 --> 15:04.560
in the administration of criminal justice. We're trying to end a life without parole

15:04.600 --> 15:07.360
sentences for children. We're trying to do something about the death penalty. We're trying

15:07.360 --> 15:11.160
to reduce the prison population. We're trying to end mass incarceration. I gave her my whole

15:11.160 --> 15:16.440
wrap, and when I finished, she looked at me, and she said, mm, mm, mm. She said, that's

15:16.440 --> 15:22.680
going to make you tired, tired, tired. And that's when Ms. Carr leaned forward. She put

15:22.680 --> 15:29.440
her finger on my face, and she said, that's why you've got to be brave, brave, brave.

15:29.440 --> 15:35.400
And I actually believe that the TED community needs to be more courageous. We need to find

15:35.400 --> 15:42.460
ways to embrace these challenges, these problems, the suffering, because ultimately, our humanity

15:42.460 --> 15:47.240
depends on everyone's humanity. I've learned very simple things doing the work that I do.

15:47.240 --> 15:52.600
It's just taught me very simple things. I've come to understand and to believe that each

15:52.600 --> 15:58.200
of us is more than the worst thing we've ever done. I believe that for every person on the

15:58.200 --> 16:03.560
planet, I think if somebody tells a lie, they're not just a liar. I think if somebody takes

16:03.560 --> 16:07.040
something that doesn't belong to them, they're not just a thief. I think even if you kill

16:07.040 --> 16:12.160
someone, you're not just a killer. And because of that, there's this basic human dignity

16:12.160 --> 16:19.160
that must be respected by law. I also believe that in many parts of this country, and certainly

16:19.160 --> 16:25.760
in many parts of this globe, that the opposite of poverty is not wealth. I don't believe

16:25.760 --> 16:33.320
it. I actually think in too many places, the opposite of poverty is justice. And finally,

16:33.320 --> 16:40.080
I believe that despite the fact that it is so dramatic and so beautiful and so inspiring

16:40.080 --> 16:46.600
and so stimulating, we will ultimately not be judged by our technology. We won't be judged

16:46.600 --> 16:52.540
by our design. We won't be judged by our intellect and reason. Ultimately, you judge the character

16:52.540 --> 16:56.980
of a society, not by how they treat the rich and the powerful and the privileged, but by

16:56.980 --> 17:03.220
how they treat the poor, the condemned, the incarcerated, because it's in that nexus that

17:03.220 --> 17:10.580
we actually begin to understand truly profound things about who we are. I sometimes get out

17:10.580 --> 17:16.560
of balance. I'll end with this story. I sometimes push too hard. I do get tired, as we all do.

17:16.560 --> 17:21.620
Sometimes those ideas get ahead of our thinking in ways that are important. And I've been

17:21.620 --> 17:26.300
representing these kids who have been sentenced to these very harsh sentences. And I go to

17:26.300 --> 17:30.020
the jail, and I see my client who's 13 and 14, and he's been certified to stand trial

17:30.020 --> 17:34.180
as an adult. And I start thinking, well, how did that happen? How can a judge turn you

17:34.180 --> 17:38.900
into something that you're not? And the judge has certified him as an adult, but I see this

17:38.900 --> 17:42.660
kid, and I was up too late one night, and I started thinking, well, gosh, if the judge

17:42.660 --> 17:47.180
can turn you into something that you're not, the judge must have magic power. I said, yeah,

17:47.180 --> 17:51.100
Brian, the judge has some magic power. You should ask for some of that. And because I

17:51.100 --> 17:54.860
was up too late and wasn't thinking real straight, I started working on a motion. And

17:54.860 --> 17:59.020
I had a client who was 14 years old, a young, poor, black kid, and started working on this

17:59.020 --> 18:04.100
motion, and the head of the motion was a motion to try my poor 14-year-old black male client

18:04.100 --> 18:14.580
like a privileged white 75-year-old corporate executive. And I put in my motion that there

18:14.580 --> 18:18.400
was prosecutorial misconduct and police misconduct and judicial misconduct. There was a crazy

18:18.400 --> 18:22.280
line in there about how there's no conduct in this county, it's all misconduct. And the

18:22.280 --> 18:26.180
next morning, I woke up, and I thought, now, did I dream that crazy motion, or did I actually

18:26.180 --> 18:34.440
write it? And to my horror, not only had I written it, but I had sent it to court. A

18:34.440 --> 18:41.400
couple months went by, and I just had forgotten all about it. And I finally decided, oh, gosh,

18:41.400 --> 18:44.640
I've got to go to the court and do this crazy case. And I got in my car, and I was feeling

18:44.640 --> 18:49.160
really overwhelmed, overwhelmed. And I got in my car, and I went to this courthouse,

18:49.160 --> 18:53.080
and I was sitting, oh, this is going to be so difficult, so painful. And I finally got

18:53.080 --> 18:56.160
out of the car, and I started walking up to the courthouse. And as I was walking up the

18:56.160 --> 19:00.320
steps of this courthouse, there was an older black man who was the janitor in this courthouse.

19:00.320 --> 19:04.640
And when this man saw me, he came over to me, and he said, who are you? I said, I'm

19:04.640 --> 19:09.080
a lawyer. He said, you're a lawyer? I said, yes, sir. And this man came over to me, and

19:09.080 --> 19:15.720
he hugged me. And he whispered in my ear, he said, I'm so proud of you. And I have to

19:15.720 --> 19:22.800
tell you, it was energizing. It connected deeply with something in me about identity,

19:22.800 --> 19:28.400
about the capacity of every person to contribute to community, to perspective that is hopeful.

19:28.400 --> 19:31.960
Well, I went into the courtroom. As soon as I walked inside, the judge saw me coming in.

19:31.960 --> 19:35.960
He said, Mr. Stevenson, did you write this crazy motion? I said, yes, sir, I did. And

19:35.960 --> 19:39.640
we started arguing. People started coming in because they were just outraged. I'd written

19:39.640 --> 19:44.280
these crazy things. And police officers were coming in, and assistant prosecutors and clerk

19:44.280 --> 19:47.600
workers. And before I knew it, the courtroom was filled with people angry that we were

19:47.600 --> 19:53.400
talking about race, that we were talking about poverty, that we were talking about inequality.

19:53.400 --> 19:56.560
And out of the corner of my eye, I could see this janitor pacing back and forth. And he

19:56.560 --> 19:59.140
kept looking through the window, and he could hear all of this hollering. He kept pacing

19:59.140 --> 20:03.400
back and forth. And finally, this older black man with this very worried look on his face

20:03.400 --> 20:08.560
came into the courtroom and sat down behind me, almost at counsel table. About 10 minutes

20:08.560 --> 20:11.360
later, the judge said we would take a break. And during the break, there was a deputy sheriff

20:11.360 --> 20:16.080
who was offended that the janitor had come into court. And this deputy jumped up, and

20:16.080 --> 20:20.080
he ran over to this older black man. He said, Jimmy, what are you doing in this courtroom?

20:20.080 --> 20:25.120
This older black man stood up, and he looked at that deputy, and he looked at me. And he

20:25.120 --> 20:31.200
said, I came into this courtroom to tell this young man, keep your eyes on the prize. Hold

20:31.200 --> 20:37.360
on. I've come to TED because I believe that many of you understand that the moral arc

20:37.360 --> 20:42.640
of the universe is long, but it bends toward justice, that we cannot be full, evolved human

20:42.640 --> 20:47.520
beings until we care about human rights and basic dignity, that all of our survival is

20:47.520 --> 20:53.200
tied to the survival of everyone, that our visions of technology and design and entertainment

20:53.200 --> 20:59.680
and creativity have to be married with visions of humanity, compassion, and justice. And

20:59.680 --> 21:04.080
more than anything, for those of you who share that, I've simply come to tell you

21:04.080 --> 21:08.080
that to keep your eyes on the prize, hold on. Thank you very much.

21:08.080 --> 21:36.000
So you heard and saw an obvious desire by this audience, this community, to help you

21:36.000 --> 21:41.840
on your way and to do something on this issue. Other than writing a check, what could we

21:41.840 --> 21:46.180
do? Well, there are opportunities all around us. If you live in the state of California,

21:46.180 --> 21:50.080
for example, there is a referendum coming up this spring where actually there's going

21:50.080 --> 21:54.040
to be an effort to redirect some of the money we spend on the politics of punishment. For

21:54.040 --> 21:59.320
example, here in California, we're going to spend $1 billion on the death penalty in the

21:59.320 --> 22:06.840
next five years. $1 billion. And yet, 46% of all homicide cases don't result in arrests.

22:06.840 --> 22:11.340
56% of all rape cases don't result. So there's an opportunity to change that. And this referendum

22:11.340 --> 22:17.400
would propose having those dollars go to law enforcement and safety. And I think that opportunity

22:17.400 --> 22:21.320
exists all around us. Now, there's been this huge decline in crime

22:21.320 --> 22:26.220
in America over the last three decades. And part of the narrative of that is sometimes

22:26.220 --> 22:31.100
that it's about increased incarceration rates. What would you say to someone who believed

22:31.100 --> 22:35.260
that? Well, actually, the violent crime rate has remained relatively stable. The great

22:35.260 --> 22:39.680
increase in mass incarceration in this country wasn't really in violent crime categories.

22:39.680 --> 22:43.940
It was this misguided war on drugs. That's where the dramatic increases have come in

22:43.940 --> 22:50.140
our prison population. And we got carried away with the rhetoric of punishment. And

22:50.140 --> 22:54.980
so we have three strikes laws that put people in prison forever for stealing a bicycle,

22:54.980 --> 22:58.980
for low-level property crimes, rather than making them give those resources back to the

22:58.980 --> 23:03.100
people who they victimized. I believe we need to do more to help people who are victimized

23:03.100 --> 23:08.220
by crime, not do less. And I think our current punishment philosophy does nothing for no

23:08.220 --> 23:12.060
one. And I think that's the orientation that we have to change.

23:12.060 --> 23:18.080
Brian, you've struck a massive chord here. You're an inspiring person. Thank you so much

23:18.080 --> 23:19.080
for coming today. Thank you very much.

23:19.080 --> 23:19.580
Thank you very much for coming today. Thank you.

